-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

I have uploaded to sunsite an ALPHA release of Yard, a suite of Perl
scripts for creating and checking rescue disks that use the new compressed
ramdisk option.

Its location at the moment is :
	sunsite.unc.edu:/pub/Linux/Incoming/yard-1.0.tar.gz

Its eventual home should be /pub/Linux/system/Recovery

For those who know what rescue disks are, and the existing packages available
for creating them, here are the fundamental features of Yard:

- - Yard has a configurable file set (like SAR-disk's) so you can specify
  the utilities you want.  You can use portions of your existing disk
  structure, and you can specify links, file substitutions, wildcards, etc.

- - Yard does numerous basic checks on the file set to ensure that the rescue
  disk will be usable.  This is the main reason I wrote the package.
  This diagnosis saves time and is more informative than 
  "init: segmentation fault".  Specifically, Yard:

	- Checks symbolic link chains and adds link files if they don't
	  already exist.  Performs a final check for unresolved links.

	- Checks for shared library dependencies of the chosen binaries.
	  Adds the libraries and the necessary loader(s).
	  Performs a final ldconfig to rebuild the ld.so.cache.
	
	- Checks common system files (eg inittab, fstab, rc) for
	  simple mistakes.  Checks that necessary executables exist on rescue
	  disk.  Checks passwd and warns of missing shells and homedirs.

	- Checks scripts to make sure interpreters are included.


- - Yard exploits the compressed ramdisk feature of 1.3.48+ kernels.
  With this you can get a kernel image plus a fairly complete root
  filesystem on a single disk; about 2.8 meg altogether.  I can get a
  feature-bloated 1.3.59 kernel plus 2.4 megs of utilities onto a
  single disk.

- - Yard doesn't use dialog, ncurses or any other menu interface.



REQUIREMENTS

	- Linux kernel that includes compressed ramdisk option (ie,
	  1.3.48 or greater).  If you have /dev/ram, you're set.

	- Perl version 5 or higher.  Version 4 will not work.
	  To see what you have, type "perl -v".

	- Root access.


May you never need it,
- -Tom


