-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

Hello,

Vital, Inc is pleased to announce the pre-release of:

			Highway To Help
			===============

'Highway  to  Help'  or H2H for short is a new product offering
that  will  let software companies  provide HyperText help with
their product on various Unix and Windows platforms at a very
reasonable price.

Users will now be able to write their help systems using any of
the  Windows  based  help  authoring tools, export it to an RTF
format, and H2H takes over from their.

H2H can also take documents written in HTML and create a 
distributable help system, i.e. create a single .hlp file which
can then be viewed with the H2H viewer.

It  is  time  to do a sanity check on all the work that we have
done  on  this  product. We are looking for some companies who
would be interested in creating distributable HyperText help for
their software products, and would like to evaluate H2H.

Some of the functionality that H2H provides:

	A hypertext help viewer / browser on all Unix platforms
	-  similar  to  Windows 95. This viewer will be able to
	display  compiled  RTF  files.  You can now create your
	entire context sensitive help using MS-Word or any Help
	editor, and then simply compile them into a sibgle .hlp
	file,  and then view it using H2H viewer. Documents can
	include tables, multiple cross-references, and graphics
	(including   support   for   Microsoft's   undocumented
	segmented  help  graphics  files,  which  allow  you to
	create hot-spot regions on top of bitmaps).

	The  help  compiler  is  fast, and creates smaller help
	files  than  either  Microsoft Windows or any competing
	product.  The  help  compiler generates reports such as
	topics  which are never referred to, or hypertext jumps
	to targets which have not been defined.

	Full  support  for  free-text  searching  of topics. (A
	Windows/95 feature).

	A  compiler to compile help projects into a single .hlp
	file,  so  that  software  developers can ship one .hlp
	file with the H2H viewer.

	A  decompiler to take an existing .hlp file, and create
	multiple  RTF  files or .HTML files. This allows you to
	conveniently translate RTF files to HTML or vice versa.
	(H2H  will  automatically  convert  bitmap  formats  as
	necessary;  e.g.  Windows  does  not support GIF or XPM
	files,  so  H2H will convert them to the equivalent BMP
	files).

	H2H   compiler  will  be  able  to  create  a  complete
	Hypertext  help  environment  for  developers  who have
	written  their documentation in HTML. The compiler will
	be  able to create a single .hlp file from the multiple
	.html  files,  and then you will be able to use the H2H
	viewer  to  provide  a  full  featured  Hypertext  help
	environment.  The H2H product allows mixing of .RTF and
	.HTML files.

	H2H viewer currently supports the following image formats:
	
	.BMP	Windows bitmaps 1, 4, and 8-bit depths

	.GIF	The universal Internet standard. Very small 
		bitmap files.

	.XPM	Useful for applications which have toolbar icons
		in this format, so you can use them directly with
		no conversion.

	.SHG	Windows proprietary segmented-graphics files. Used
		so you can do something like have a screen snapshot
		of your application and jump to different hypertext
		links depending on what part of the screen image the
		user clicks on.

	A tool to aid in the conversion of nroff/troff files to RTF
	format.


Cheers
