                    SAS Institute Delivers 3270 Emulation
                       Emulation Software for Linux

CARY, NC (February 22, 1996) -- SAS Institute today announces 
availability of its popular 3270 terminal emulation software,
Emulus(r), for the Linux operating system on Intel-based hardware.

Emulus is an X-Windows/Motif application which uses TCP/IP to
establish a connection to an IBM mainframe host, emulating a
3270 terminal.  Emulus includes the following features:

  * mainframe graphics support (vector graphics and programmed-symbol
    graphics); graphics are supported for any screen size; 16 colors
    are supported

  * 3270 data stream extensions provide for additional graphics
    interactivity with SAS applications such as SAS/INSIGHT, the
    Graphics Editor in SAS/GRAPH, and the Frame Entry in SAS/AF

  * complete customization through dialog windows

  * scripting facility to record and playback host interaction
 
  * EasyPads allow the user to build customize windows of buttons;
    EasyPads can be made into a pull-down menu, or a toolbar.

  * cut and paste between other X applications and Emulus

  * many other standard 3270 features and extensions

Included with Emulus is Helplus, an interactive, hypertext help system
modeled after Microsoft's WinHelp.  It is used to provide on-line
Help for Emulus, but can also be used to build Help files for use
with other X applications.  It accepts the same input as WinHelp
(namely help project files and RTF source), provides graphics support
(Helplus will read either XPM or GIF files), and also supports standard
WinHelp features such as secondary windows, pop-up topics, topic
annotations and bookmarks, and most WinHelp macros.

Emulus is available for Linux on CD-ROM for a cost of $99.  Included is
a hardcopy book and one year of technical support.  

Emulus is also commercially available for HP-UX, AIX/6000 and Solaris 1
and Solaris 2 Unix systems.  On these platforms, it can be licensed on
an annual basis and renewed yearly, or purchased as shrink-wrapped.
To order a copy, or for more information, call SAS Institute's Book 
Sales division at (919) 677-8000.  Or visit us on the Web, at
http://www.sas.com

======================================================================

Some other points:

  * The importance of support for mainframe graphics cannot be
    understated.  Most available 3270 emulators (commercial or
    public domain) either do not support mainframe graphics, or
    do so poorly.  This is Emulus' main strength.  

    The 3270 datastream extensions alluded to above are also
    important, but only for Emulus users running SAS mainframe
    applications.  The extensions provide workstation-like capability
    for mainframe SAS applications, including backing store for
    graphics, color loading by RGB value, rubber-banding, and
    dynamic graphics cursor shapes.

  * Helplus is a signficant program in its own right.  Included with
    Helplus is Shed, modeled after Microsoft's "shed.exe", which can
    be used to create segmented graphics, in which different areas
    of a picture can be linked to different Help topics.

    Helplus is both a compiler and viewer.  In compilation mode,
    it reads a help project file, which specifies which RTF
    source files are needed to build the help file.  In viewer mode,
    it displays the given help file, but also runs as a help server,
    servicing requests to view other help files created with Helplus.

    Helplus uses the same API (a call to the "WinHelp" function) as
    Windows applications use.  As such, it can be used to provide
    context-sensitive help for other X applications written in C.
    The necessary object files to connect an application with a 
    Helplus server are provided.

    Helplus supports most WinHelp macros, but also provides some
    macros unique to Helplus.  For example, the ClientMessage macro
    can be used to communicate from the Helplus server back to the
    application (this allows advanced tutorials to be constructed).
    Helplus also supports full text search, although this is not
    as robust as WinHelp 4.0, since Helplus is modeled after the
    Windows 3.1 version of WinHelp (but I'm working on this).  Helplus
    does support some WinHelp 4 features, such as the ability to
    place actual working push-buttons in help topic text.

    Helplus is very similar to Bristol's HyperHelp product.  It is
    superior in it's look and feel (i.e., it more closely resembles
    WinHelp), but probably is not as robust in its support of 
    RTF (but very nice help files can nonetheless be built with
    it, evidenced by viewing the help files that come with the
    product).  Helplus can compile RTF files produced by ForeHelp,
    a Windows-based help authoring tool (it has less success with
    files produced by RoboHelp, and even less with RTF files generated
    by Microsoft Word).

    Three Helplus help files are included--one for Emulus, one for
    Helplus itself, and one help file documenting how to create
    help files with Helplus.

  * Emulus, Helplus and Shed are Motif applications.  SAS Institute
    maintains a Motif source license, so Motif is statically linked
    with each of these executables.  The user does not have to have
    Motif for Linux.

  * Emulus and supporting applications and files require about 
    5M of disk space.  The executables were built with a 1.2 Linux
    kernel, and are not ELF.  They should run though with other
    kernel versions.  

  * Emulus requires TCP/IP on the IBM mainframe host.



