I'm looking for someone to take over the maintenance and updating of
the various Linux HOWTOs which I currently maintain. Perhaps "maintain"
is too strong a word---I haven't had time to even look at these documents
for some time, and I don't anticipate having the time soon, either.

I'm looking for maintainers for the Linux Installation HOWTO (currently
covering Slackware 2.x, over a year old) and the Linux XFree86 HOWTO
(covering XFree86 3.1; should not be hard to update for the newest version).
It is possible to maintain both documents, or you can volunteer to maintain
only one.

What does this job entail? Essentially you will maintain the Linuxdoc-SGML 
source code for one or both HOWTOs. Updates are sent to Greg Hankins 
(gregh@cc.gatech.edu), the Linux HOWTO maintainer; he automatically 
generates the various formats (plain ASCII, HTML, etc.) and archives 
them for you; you just send him a new SGML file whenever you make an
update. The HOWTOs are written in the Linuxdoc-SGML format, which is
fairly easy to learn (it looks a lot like a hybrid of HTML and LaTeX).

What requirements are there for the job? First, you should be fairly 
knowledgeable about Linux (at least a year's background using and 
maintaining the system). Secondly, you should be able to write technical
material lucidly, at a level where absolute beginners to Linux have no
problem following along. These two HOWTOs are two of the most important
introductory documents to the system---literally _everyone_ who installs
Linux for the first time reads one or both of these docs. The clarity and
accuracy of these HOWTOs are really important for the popularity and
acceptance of Linux on the large scale.

The most important requirement is that you have the time to actively
maintain these documents, answer questions from readers, incorporate 
updates and bugfixes, and actually keep them up-to-date (unlike I have
been able to do). I don't want to hand these off to someone who is
as busy as I am ... 

What will you get out of it? Well... marginal fame, possible fortune,
questionable sex-appeal, unlikely prestige, but most certainly a lot
of e-mail who want to thank you. These documents are also published
in a number of places---apart from books such as 'The Linux Bible',
the Installation and XFree86 HOWTOs have been incorporated as part of
three or four other well-known books in the computer industry (usually
as appendices). They are also distributed as part of every major Linux
CD-ROM out there, often as the only installation instructions on the CD.
Just having your name in print might be worth the effort!

If interested, please contact me via e-mail at mdw@cs.cornell.edu. 
I'll choose the maintainer(s) for these documents fairly subjectively
(mostly based upon your reply and track record, if any). If I know you, 
that's a big bonus. Sorry, I do not accept major credit cards.

Thanks,
M. Welsh
