Subject: Passing the torch of the Linux docs
Date: 1996/02/22
Message-ID: <4gjd79$o8s@thokk.cs.cornell.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 140718767
followup-to: comp.os.linux.misc
organization: Cornell CS Robotics and Vision Laboratory, Ithaca, NY 14850
keywords: Linux Documentation Project
newsgroups: comp.os.linux.announce,comp.os.linux.misc

Friends,

With the release of version 2.3 of `Linux Installation and Getting
Started', and my request to find new maintainers for my old Linux HOWTOs,
I'm detaching myself from active work with the Linux Documentation Project
and helping to maintain free documents about this operating system. 

There's really no reason for this other than that I have little or no
time to continue working on this project---I'd much rather make it
possible for others to pick up where I'm leaving off. For the last year
or so I've been far too swamped with my research career and full-time
work as a student. I don't want to let Linux docs die completely by
`sitting on' an idle project for too long!

Your encouragement and support over the years has really contributed to
the amazing quantity of free documentation that is out there. The Linux
Documentation Project was started by Lars Wirzenius, Michael K. Johnson,
and myself almost four years ago, and since then it has snowballed into an 
amazing collaborative effort resulting in _thousands_ of pages of free
documentation available for this system. I would offer each of you a beer
to express my gratitude, but I don't have quite enough credit line. :)

As I'm `passing the torch' and trying to make it possible for others
to modify and re-release my docs, I'd like to make a few simple requests
for the future.

	- Note that the I&GS is now covered by the GPL. This means that
	  anyone can make translations and derivative works. This is
	  certainly intentional and I hope it works well.

	- Please try to keep new versions of these docs consistent and
	  use unambiguous version schemes. If everyone makes small
	  changes to these docs and re-releases them, we'll have twelve
	  inconsistent copies where it will be unclear which is 
	  derived from which. Note that under the GPL, you are required
	  to release source code if you make changes to these docs.

	- I'd encourage someone to step forward and act as the 'maintainer'
	  or 'librarian' for the "official" version of the I&GS. This
	  "official" version should hopefully incorporate changes from
	  everyone else. 

	- Do keep all of the original copyright notices and licenses 
	  entact, including proper attribution to the original author.

	- By all means have fun, live long, and prosper.

It's doubtful that I'm going to phase out of the Linux community 
altogether (sorry to let you down) --- currently I'm working with Linux
in the context of high-performance systems and networking research.
Check out http://www.cs.cornell.edu/home/mdw/ if you're interested;
I'm also gathering a Web page of links to other projects. Also, look
for a Second Edition of the O'Reilly book (_Running_Linux_) sometime
in the not-too-distant future.

Thanks to everyone for your comments, ideas, and help over the years.
It's been a fun ride.

Happy hacking!
mdw

