Version 1.3 of the Karma library is now available for ftp. The Karma
library is a general purpose programmers toolkit (see below).

	Karma is available for anonymous ftp from:  ftp.atnf.csiro.au
  under the  pub/software/karma  directory. Both the source code and binary
  distributions are kept here. The source code is available free of charge.
  There is also a WWW page for the library:
    http://wwwatnf.atnf.csiro.au/karma/
  In addition, the ATNF visualisation software, which uses the Karma
  library, also has a WWW page:
    http://wwwatnf.atnf.csiro.au/Software/Analysis/visualisation/

  Binary distributions for:
	SunSparc, SunOS 4.1.3
	SunSparc, Solaris 2.4
	i386, Linux 1.2.8 and 1.3.x (ELF)
	Convex C2, ConvexOS 10.1.2    (incomplete: X11 applications missing)
	mips1, Ultrix V4.3 (Rev. 44)
	alpha, OSF/1 V2.0 (Rev. 240)
	hp9000, HP-UX A.09.01
	mips4, IRIX 6.0.1
	rs6000, AIX 3.2
  are available in addition to the source distribution.

Notable changes since 1.2:

1)  Creation of the <foreign_> package. This provides for conversion
to and from foreign data formats. Currently supported formats are:
PPM (Portable PixMap), FITS (Flexible Image Transport System), Sun
Rasterfile and Miriad Image files.

2)  Move away from <win_scale_struct> for the <canvas_> package
towards attribute lists has commenced.

3)  Tiled image display now fully supported for PseudoColour images.

4)  Restructured <kwin_> package to ease support of different graphics
systems. XGL stereo canvases now supported. OpenGL stereo canvases
planned.

5)  Formatted printing to channel objects now supported through
<ch_printf> routine.

6)  Support for Associative Arrays.

7)  Support for background processing through "work functions". Note
this is not the same as thread support (already provided in Karma).

8)  New <vrender_> package to support volume rendering. This package
handles most of work in implementing a volume rendering tool, leaving
the programmer free to develop the GUI and shading algorithms.
Experimental.

9)  Extensions to <mt_> package to provide a few different methods for
synchronising multiple threads of execution.

10)  New, improved volume rendering tool <xray> included in the binary
distributions. See the ATNF visualisation WWW page for information.

11)  Various extra features, bugfixes and optimisations.

12)  Improved tutorials/examples!

See the release notes for more details. Below are README extracts:

===============================================================================
				Karma

		    A powerful programmers toolkit


	Karma is a toolkit for interprocess communications,
  authentication, encryption, graphics display, user interface and
  manipulating the Karma network data structure. It contains KarmaLib
  (the structured libraries and API) and a large number of modules
  (applications) to perform many standard tasks.

  When files and directories are referred to, they are given relative to the
  root Karma directory (environment variable KARMABASE if defined). This is
  usually  /usr/local/karma
  Please see the file  INSTALL  for instructions on how to install Karma.

  The update policy for Karma is in the file  doc/update-policy

  The  doc/lib  directory contains documentation for the Karma libraries.

  The  doc/modules  directory contains documentation for the various Karma
  modules. This directory may be a little sparse...

  The  doc/howto  directory contains information on how to make good use of
  some of the more interesting packages in KarmaLib.

  The  doc/widgets  directory contains information on the Karma Widgets
  library. This directory may be a little sparse...

	Please mail any bug reports to:  karma-bugs@atnf.csiro.au
  No warranty, express or implied should be inferred by this mailing address.

	If you want to be added to the mailing list (to find out what's changed
  with Karma), please mail the request to:  karma-request@atnf.csiro.au
  This mailing list is VERY important: as bugs are fixed and enhancements made
  to Karma, this is your only way of finding out about these changes.

	If you have any suggestions for Karma, please mail them to:
  karma-suggestions@atnf.csiro.au

	Karma is available for anonymous ftp from:  ftp.atnf.csiro.au
  under the  pub/software/karma  directory. Both the source code and binary
  distributions are kept here. The source code is available free of charge.
  There is also a WWW page for the library:
    http://wwwatnf.atnf.csiro.au/karma/
  In addition, the ATNF visualisation software, which uses the Karma
  library, also has a WWW page:
    http://wwwatnf.atnf.csiro.au/Software/Analysis/visualisation/

  The Karma World Wide Web page is:  http://wwwatnf.atnf.csiro.au/karma/
  This provides online access to the documentation and ftp site.

  You are urged to get the binary distribution if possible, as this will save
  you the effort of building from source.
  When connecting to the ftp server, check the modification dates on the source
  distribution and the binary distribution. If the source distribution
  is much newer than the binary distribution you want, this means that
  the binary distribution is out of date. Please send an Email request to
